<speak version="1.0" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2001/10/synthesis" xml:lang="en-US"><prosody rate="+18%">In this town, you only get a hundred words a day. My number floats right over my head. At zero, your voice just stops. So every morning, Mom says, "Save your words, Jay." At school, we talk with our hands. The bakery sells "yes" and "no" cards. Two bucks each. Except Tyler, who never shuts up. His number never goes under ninety. The principal says he's just lucky, I guess. Grandpa's number has said zero all week. The mayor says old people stop getting new words. People kinda stopped visiting. He can't answer anyway. Last summer, he told me one thing. "Words don't run out, Jay. They move." Then a letter came from town hall. Seven days at zero, and your voice never comes back. Grandpa was on day six. Then my teacher yelled at me. Like, twelve words. And my number went up. Twelve more. The number only counts words people say to you. <break time="800ms"/> Tyler wasn't lucky. Everybody talks to Tyler. So yeah, nobody talks to Grandpa. I rang the school bell and ran. My whole class followed me to his porch. Forty kids, all talking at once. His number went ten, fifty, a hundred. Grandpa looked at me and said, "They move." Mom laughed on the steps. "Save your words," she said. Then I looked at her number. <break time="900ms"/> It said two.</prosody></speak>